        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-22T01:16:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The threat \"1. The Real Security Threat: \"invented\" Writes (Store Spilling)\" in one of your comments, pointed that, if register pressure is high, the compiler can use the shared variables as scratch space during the function — storing unrelated intermediate values there temporarily - because it \"knows\" it will overwrite the location later anyway. A concurrent reader can observe sensitive hypervisor data. So I understand this is the single-threaded as-if rule. Based on this, we need volatile write-back?\n\n\u003e (Otherwise we should change such writes to WRITE_ONCE in other such places as well?)\n\nAs we discussed, we will have another patch serial to replace the plain assignment with READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE, so we can cover such places in that patch serial.",
      "parentUuid": "2e136555_ee73b0ab",
      "revId": "afa0cf57247a4862d8567e98be0964a8400951a9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
